WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.660 --> 00:00:01.493
- [Narrator] This might look

00:00:01.493 --> 00:00:03.223
like your typical Western background,

00:00:03.223 --> 00:00:05.590
but it's actually an abandoned set…

00:00:05.590 --> 00:00:06.653
Of a video game.

00:00:08.540 --> 00:00:11.360
- The Western was easy to
accomplish here in New Mexico.

00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:13.937
We have the sets, we have the
cowboys, we have the horses.

00:00:13.937 --> 00:00:15.853
We had everything we needed.

00:00:17.730 --> 00:00:20.310
- [Narrator] Including a great villain.

00:00:20.310 --> 00:00:22.367
Mad Dog McCree.

00:00:22.367 --> 00:00:25.263
(evil laughter)

00:00:25.263 --> 00:00:28.850
- There had never been a
live-action interactive

00:00:28.850 --> 00:00:32.783
video arcade game, until
Mad Dog came along.

00:00:37.218 --> 00:00:40.135
(video game music)

00:00:41.270 --> 00:00:43.220
- [Narrator] The year was 1990,

00:00:43.220 --> 00:00:46.970
the dawn of a new age of videos
games was about to begin.

00:00:46.970 --> 00:00:50.800
Arcades had small but powerful
rivals in the living room.

00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:52.249
The smallest, the Game Boy.

00:00:52.249 --> 00:00:54.620
Born in 1989.

00:00:54.620 --> 00:00:58.610
The scariest, the home
console, SEGA 16-bit Genesis

00:00:58.610 --> 00:01:01.170
and Nintendo's Super NES.

00:01:01.170 --> 00:01:03.810
Now, anyone can play high-end video games,

00:01:03.810 --> 00:01:05.268
anywhere they wanted.

00:01:05.268 --> 00:01:07.340
Arcade makers had to step up

00:01:07.340 --> 00:01:09.290
and utilize their main advantage.

00:01:09.290 --> 00:01:12.860
More storage space that could
hold hours of video footage.

00:01:12.860 --> 00:01:14.830
Instead of computer generated graphics,

00:01:14.830 --> 00:01:18.750
they used live-action, live
dialogue, and live actors.

00:01:18.750 --> 00:01:19.990
The type of production values

00:01:19.990 --> 00:01:22.340
that console games couldn't touch.

00:01:22.340 --> 00:01:24.470
Which brings us back to where we started,

00:01:24.470 --> 00:01:28.050
New Mexico, a place where
a one of a kind experiment

00:01:28.050 --> 00:01:30.423
changed the video game industry, forever.

00:01:32.710 --> 00:01:35.850
The props from that first live-
action game, still remain,

00:01:35.850 --> 00:01:38.283
in a dusty warehouse, outside Albuquerque.

00:01:39.428 --> 00:01:41.720
(coughing)

00:01:41.720 --> 00:01:45.283
- I'm Dave Roberts, and I was
director on Mad Dog McCree.

00:01:46.130 --> 00:01:47.710
Hands up.

00:01:47.710 --> 00:01:51.713
It was our first entry into video games.

00:01:52.798 --> 00:01:55.940
- These are the fake dynamite sticks.

00:01:55.940 --> 00:01:58.390
- [Narrator] The makers of
Mad Dog weren't always in the

00:01:58.390 --> 00:02:00.260
business of making video games.

00:02:00.260 --> 00:02:02.450
They actually got their
start making police training

00:02:02.450 --> 00:02:03.373
simulators.

00:02:06.390 --> 00:02:09.330
- We shot things that police
would encounter during

00:02:09.330 --> 00:02:10.260
their day,

00:02:10.260 --> 00:02:12.390
they put it up on a big screen,

00:02:12.390 --> 00:02:14.660
and the police officer
would walk into room,

00:02:14.660 --> 00:02:17.360
he would have a pistol that
had a laser put into it,

00:02:17.360 --> 00:02:19.520
and he would respond to
whatever was happening

00:02:19.520 --> 00:02:21.014
on the screen.

00:02:21.014 --> 00:02:23.090
Once we knew how to do that,

00:02:23.090 --> 00:02:25.663
it made it a little easier
to do Mad Dog McCree.

00:02:29.770 --> 00:02:32.530
- My name is Robert Grebe,
and I was the CEO of

00:02:32.530 --> 00:02:34.700
American Laser Games.

00:02:34.700 --> 00:02:37.540
This is the aha moment, actually.

00:02:37.540 --> 00:02:40.330
Looking at this picture, I
realized that there was a

00:02:40.330 --> 00:02:44.020
potential to turn this
simulator into a video game.

00:02:44.020 --> 00:02:44.853
So,

00:02:46.360 --> 00:02:49.953
that became Mad Dog, Mad Dog McCree.

00:02:53.170 --> 00:02:54.960
- [Narrator] The team now
had the technology to make a

00:02:54.960 --> 00:02:56.190
video game.

00:02:56.190 --> 00:02:58.520
But what they needed was a story.

00:02:58.520 --> 00:03:00.240
Being located in New Mexico,

00:03:00.240 --> 00:03:02.810
the Wild West was an obvious choice.

00:03:02.810 --> 00:03:04.370
And they didn't have to go too far

00:03:04.370 --> 00:03:06.008
to find their stars.

00:03:06.008 --> 00:03:08.675
(western music)

00:03:11.940 --> 00:03:13.290
- My name is Russ Dillen,

00:03:13.290 --> 00:03:14.957
and I played Mad Dog McCree.

00:03:16.929 --> 00:03:18.600
I’ve lived in New Mexico all my life,

00:03:18.600 --> 00:03:21.317
I was born and raised here on this ranch.

00:03:21.317 --> 00:03:23.482
I've been out here for 60 years.

00:03:23.482 --> 00:03:25.910
We started shooting Mad Dog,

00:03:25.910 --> 00:03:27.660
nobody knew who Mad Dog was,

00:03:27.660 --> 00:03:29.217
or who it was gonna be.

00:03:29.217 --> 00:03:32.840
Bob Grebe decided I would
make a good Mad Dog.

00:03:32.840 --> 00:03:34.565
Obviously not for my looks.

00:03:34.565 --> 00:03:37.403
Probably was ’cause I could quick draw.

00:03:37.403 --> 00:03:39.053
I was pretty quick in those days.

00:03:41.290 --> 00:03:43.330
- [Narrator] Finding a Mad
Dog who could quick draw,

00:03:43.330 --> 00:03:44.485
was easy.

00:03:44.485 --> 00:03:48.010
But transforming a blond-
haired, blue-eyed rancher,

00:03:48.010 --> 00:03:51.030
into a rough and tumble
outlaw form the Wild West,

00:03:51.030 --> 00:03:52.661
that's a different story.

00:03:52.661 --> 00:03:55.810
- I guess the most time
consuming part was the makeup

00:03:55.810 --> 00:03:57.350
and the hair stuff.

00:03:57.350 --> 00:03:59.210
And that hair dye's a mess.

00:03:59.210 --> 00:04:00.380
If I'd sweat, you know,

00:04:00.380 --> 00:04:03.103
and do this, it'd be
black stuff rolling down

00:04:03.103 --> 00:04:04.643
my forehead.

00:04:06.260 --> 00:04:08.370
- When I saw him at
first dressed as Mad Dog,

00:04:08.370 --> 00:04:10.143
I had no clue it was my husband.

00:04:10.990 --> 00:04:13.693
It was kind of like
looking at somebody else.

00:04:14.825 --> 00:04:16.778
- I feel like it's sticking
really well, isn't it?

00:04:16.778 --> 00:04:20.100
I was dressed in everything black,

00:04:20.100 --> 00:04:22.439
and that horrid orange scarf,

00:04:22.439 --> 00:04:25.342
looks almost like a carnival thing, but

00:04:25.342 --> 00:04:27.470
I guess that became the
trademark of Mad Dog,

00:04:27.470 --> 00:04:28.633
was that orange scarf.

00:04:32.440 --> 00:04:34.190
- [Narrator] The plot of
Mad Dog McCree goes something

00:04:34.190 --> 00:04:35.670
like this:

00:04:35.670 --> 00:04:38.290
a gang of outlaws has laid
claim to a small town.

00:04:38.290 --> 00:04:40.510
Kidnapping the mayor, and his daughter.

00:04:40.510 --> 00:04:42.760
The player's job is to kill
the member's of the gang,

00:04:42.760 --> 00:04:45.240
and eventually, Mad Dog himself,

00:04:45.240 --> 00:04:46.743
freeing the town from terror.

00:04:47.583 --> 00:04:48.416
(screaming woman)

00:04:48.416 --> 00:04:50.980
Because players competed
against live actors in the game,

00:04:50.980 --> 00:04:54.030
the biggest challenge was
accounting for unpredictability.

00:04:54.030 --> 00:04:56.390
The crew had to shoot each scene in a way

00:04:56.390 --> 00:04:58.190
that allowed for multiple outcomes,

00:04:58.190 --> 00:04:59.996
of the same situation.

00:04:59.996 --> 00:05:02.326
- We've got four people in the shot.

00:05:02.326 --> 00:05:04.190
Who does the player shoot first?

00:05:04.190 --> 00:05:05.810
Oh my, he shoots number one first.

00:05:05.810 --> 00:05:07.840
But, what if he shot number four first?

00:05:07.840 --> 00:05:09.440
Well, we've gotta film that, too.

00:05:11.409 --> 00:05:13.649
- In one scene alone, I'm
probably four different people.

00:05:13.649 --> 00:05:15.950
I'm one of the guy that
jumps into the street

00:05:15.950 --> 00:05:17.050
and tries to shoot you,

00:05:17.050 --> 00:05:19.570
and then as you're walking
up the street, to the saloon,

00:05:19.570 --> 00:05:21.286
me and Al come out fighting.

00:05:21.286 --> 00:05:23.686
- [Director] And action.

00:05:23.686 --> 00:05:25.926
(grunting)

00:05:25.926 --> 00:05:27.923
- And stay down.

00:05:27.923 --> 00:05:29.362
And stay out.

00:05:29.362 --> 00:05:32.428
- It was pretty corny, you know.

00:05:32.428 --> 00:05:35.990
But yeah, there's always, you know, a
special place for Mad Dog,

00:05:35.990 --> 00:05:38.463
'cause of what it was and what it became.

00:05:40.940 --> 00:05:43.400
- We didn't know if the
game would be a success.

00:05:43.400 --> 00:05:45.630
We didn't know if the
product would work or not.

00:05:45.630 --> 00:05:48.110
I mean, it was on the job
training for all of us.

00:05:48.110 --> 00:05:50.126
♪ It's hard to see the light ♪

00:05:50.126 --> 00:05:52.690
♪ Shining so bright. ♪

00:05:52.690 --> 00:05:56.460
- It really exploded instantly
in popularity worldwide,

00:05:56.460 --> 00:05:59.151
'cause nobody had ever
seen anything like it.

00:05:59.151 --> 00:06:01.610
♪ You've had a long, long run. ♪

00:06:01.610 --> 00:06:02.970
- You could hardly get near a game,

00:06:02.970 --> 00:06:04.845
'cause it was just so popular.

00:06:04.845 --> 00:06:06.892
♪ Through the rain ♪

00:06:06.892 --> 00:06:09.233
♪ Through the heat ♪

00:06:09.233 --> 00:06:11.590
♪ You won't always sleep alone ♪

00:06:11.590 --> 00:06:14.001
♪ But you won’t ever stay the week ♪

00:06:14.001 --> 00:06:16.147
♪ You better run ♪

00:06:16.147 --> 00:06:17.179
- I really appreciate just how much

00:06:17.179 --> 00:06:20.563
that we accomplished back 20 years ago.

00:06:20.563 --> 00:06:24.799
- I never really thought that
Mad Dog would have a legacy.

00:06:24.799 --> 00:06:27.690
But it's iconic in that it really sort of

00:06:27.690 --> 00:06:29.560
represented this moment of time when

00:06:29.560 --> 00:06:32.340
video gaming went from Pong and Pac-Man,

00:06:32.340 --> 00:06:34.723
to Grand Theft Auto, you know.

00:06:37.170 --> 00:06:40.120
I think it was a wonderful
stepping stone for the industry.

